WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.000
Part four.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.645
Using AI without getting fooled.

00:00:05.195 --> 00:00:10.135
Give an AI assistant two spreadsheets and ask it to merge them.

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:12.695
Seconds later, you'll have a clean table.

00:00:12.695 --> 00:00:14.655
It will look perfect.

00:00:14.655 --> 00:00:17.485
And it may have quietly lost a whole state.

00:00:18.035 --> 00:00:21.605
Think of AI as a very fast intern.

00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:25.455
It has read a lot, and it has never visited the county.

00:00:25.455 --> 00:00:29.205
It's great for speed, and it needs someone checking its work.

00:00:29.205 --> 00:00:31.465
Here are four jobs it does well.

00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:35.115
One: read the fine print.

00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:39.375
The notes that explain a dataset can run for many pages.

00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:44.225
Upload them to a tool that answers from your own sources, such as Gemini Notebook,

00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:47.615
which used to be called NotebookLM.

00:00:47.615 --> 00:00:50.475
Ask: how was this number calculated?

00:00:50.475 --> 00:00:52.195
What year is it from?

00:00:52.195 --> 00:00:52.945
Who's left out?

00:00:53.495 --> 00:00:56.525
Two: match columns.

00:00:56.525 --> 00:01:00.585
Paste the column names and definitions from two tables.

00:01:00.585 --> 00:01:07.265
Ask which ones mean the same thing, and ask it to flag any that only look the same.

00:01:07.265 --> 00:01:11.285
In one clinic's system, the letter C might mean completed.

00:01:11.285 --> 00:01:13.595
In another's, canceled.

00:01:13.595 --> 00:01:16.135
A matching label isn't a matching meaning.

00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:19.733
Three: write the formula.

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:25.943
Ask for an XLOOKUP that joins two tables on FIPS code and keeps the leading zero.

00:01:25.943 --> 00:01:31.723
If you do code, ask for a short script, with every step explained in plain English.

00:01:32.287 --> 00:01:35.227
Four: draft your Source Card.

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:39.707
Give it the documentation and ask it to answer the five questions.

00:01:39.707 --> 00:01:43.204
Then you check each answer against the source.

00:01:43.754 --> 00:01:45.784
Now the checks.

00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:47.804
Do these every time.

00:01:47.804 --> 00:01:52.184
Count: do you have the same number of rows before and after?

00:01:52.184 --> 00:01:57.714
Spot-check: pick three places and look them up by hand on the original website.

00:01:57.714 --> 00:02:01.904
Ask why: make it quote the source for any definition.

00:02:01.904 --> 00:02:03.801
If it can't, don't use it.

00:02:04.352 --> 00:02:06.392
One hard rule.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:11.282
Never paste information about real patients into a public AI tool,

00:02:11.282 --> 00:02:13.332
meaning any free chatbot,

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:17.702
or any tool your organization hasn't approved for patient data.

00:02:17.702 --> 00:02:22.532
In a small community, even a few details can identify someone.

00:02:22.532 --> 00:02:25.601
The public data in this tutorial is fine to use.

00:02:26.151 --> 00:02:28.771
AI makes the work faster.

00:02:28.771 --> 00:02:33.691
You're still responsible for the result, so say how you used it.

00:02:33.691 --> 00:02:38.521
You now know why rural data is different, five questions to ask,

00:02:38.521 --> 00:02:43.101
where to find the tools, and how to use AI with care.

00:02:43.101 --> 00:02:49.511
Download the Field Guide, pick a county you care about, and fill in one Source Card.

00:02:49.511 --> 00:02:51.051
See you in the next tutorial.

